::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which choice best shows audience-aware tone?
{
~A. Use sarcasm so the audience laughs at the topic.#Sarcasm can weaken a serious safety message.
=B. Use a calm, respectful tone when explaining a serious school safety topic.#Correct. Tone should fit the serious purpose and school audience.
~C. Use confusing words so the message sounds advanced.#Confusing language does not support the audience.
~D. Use an angry tone so listeners feel blamed.#Blame can make the audience defensive.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
What should a listener do before disagreeing?
{
~A. Interrupt quickly#Interrupting prevents full understanding.
~B. Change the speaker's meaning#Changing meaning is not active listening.
=C. Paraphrase the speaker's idea accurately#Correct. Paraphrasing checks understanding before response.
~D. Ignore the speaker's purpose#Purpose helps shape a fair response.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence uses academic language clearly?
{
=A. Your evidence supports the claim, but the transition needs to be clearer.#Correct. This uses precise vocabulary and gives useful feedback.
~B. Your part has stuff that is good.#This is vague and does not name the communication skill.
~C. The thing should sound better.#This is too vague to guide revision.
~D. It is fine or whatever.#This does not use academic language or explain the idea.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "As your class representative, I have listened to your concerns." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Emotion because the speaker uses a sad story#No sad story appears.
~B. Logic because the speaker gives data#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. No appeal because the sentence is simple#Simple language can still build trust.
=D. Trust because the speaker identifies a responsible role#Correct. The speaker builds credibility by naming a role.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response gives the clearest instructional help?
{
~A. Do the thing from the page.#This is vague and does not explain the task.
~B. Write anything about speaking.#This is too broad and unhelpful.
=C. Start by naming the audience, then explain why that audience affects tone.#Correct. This gives clear steps tied to the lesson.
~D. Make it sound complete.#This does not explain what completion requires.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best builds on a classmate's idea?
{
~A. I was not listening.#This does not build on the idea.
=B. Your point about audience connects to our tone; we could make the opening more respectful.#Correct. This connects to the idea and adds a useful next step.
~C. That has nothing to do with me.#This dismisses the group task.
~D. Stop sharing ideas.#This blocks collaboration.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which sentence best matches audience and purpose?
{
~A. Little kids, do not mess this up.#This is disrespectful and harsh.
~B. Whatever, hallway safety is a thing.#This is careless and unclear.
~C. I guess you can listen if you want.#This is uncertain and weak.
=D. Please walk on the right side so everyone can move safely.#Correct. The sentence is clear, respectful, and safety-focused.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "Imagine a hallway where every student moves safely and calmly." Which appeal is strongest?
{
=A. Emotion because the speaker creates a positive image#Correct. The speaker helps the audience picture a safe and calm result.
~B. Logic because the speaker gives a statistic#No statistic appears.
~C. Trust because the speaker lists credentials#No credentials appear.
~D. No appeal because hallways are ordinary#Ordinary topics can still use rhetorical appeals.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which feedback is most precise?
{
~A. The end is nice.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
=B. The conclusion restates the claim and reminds the audience of the purpose.#Correct. This names the speech part and communication function.
~C. The last thing is okay.#This does not name the conclusion or explain why.
~D. It has words at the end.#This is not useful academic feedback.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response shows respectful disagreement?
{
~A. Your idea is wrong.#This is blunt and unsupported.
~B. My idea wins.#This is competitive, not collaborative.
=C. I understand your idea, but a serious opening may fit the safety topic better.#Correct. This paraphrases, disagrees respectfully, and gives a reason.
~D. Nobody should listen to that.#This is disrespectful.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Why should a speaker identify audience before choosing tone?
{
=A. Tone should fit who is listening and why the message matters.#Correct. Audience and purpose guide tone.
~B. Every audience needs the same tone.#Different audiences may need different tone choices.
~C. Audience has no effect on tone.#Audience strongly affects tone.
~D. Tone should be chosen randomly.#Tone should be intentional.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response best clarifies a school task?
{
~A. This is confusing.#This states confusion but does not clarify the task.
=B. Do we need to submit one paragraph or a list of bullet points?#Correct. This asks a specific question about expectations.
~C. I refuse to do it.#This does not seek understanding.
~D. Someone should explain everything.#This is too broad to guide help.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "If each person practices once, we can find timing problems before the presentation." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Emotion because the speaker creates sadness#The excerpt gives a practical reason, not a feeling.
~B. Trust because the speaker names a role#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. No appeal because the topic is practice#Practice can be explained rhetorically.
=D. Logic because the speaker explains cause and effect#Correct. Practice leads to finding timing problems.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which active listening response is strongest?
{
~A. I do not know what you said.#This does not show understanding.
~B. I disagree before you finish.#This interrupts and prevents listening.
=C. You are saying the audience needs a clear example before the evidence.#Correct. This accurately paraphrases a likely communication idea.
~D. That topic is boring.#This dismisses the speaker.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which word is most academic for information that supports a claim?
{
=A. evidence#Correct. Evidence is precise academic vocabulary.
~B. thing#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. stuff#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~D. whatever#This is informal and unclear.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which tone best fits a request for help from a Teacher of Record?
{
~A. Demanding#A demand may sound disrespectful.
~B. Sarcastic#Sarcasm does not fit a help request.
~C. Careless#A help request should be clear and responsible.
=D. Respectful and specific#Correct. The audience is a teacher and the purpose is to get help.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best repairs misunderstanding?
{
~A. You said it wrong.#This blames the speaker.
=B. I may have misunderstood. Did you mean the audience needs a simpler example?#Correct. This admits possible confusion and asks a specific question.
~C. I will pretend I understood.#Pretending does not repair misunderstanding.
~D. Your point does not matter.#This dismisses the speaker.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Which explanation connects rhetoric to purpose?
{
~A. The speaker uses words because speeches need words.#This is too general.
~B. The speaker sounds interesting.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
=C. The speaker uses a statistic to persuade the audience that more practice time is needed.#Correct. This connects evidence to persuasive purpose.
~D. The speaker has a topic.#This does not explain a rhetorical choice.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which answer gives clear next steps?
{
~A. Do some speech stuff.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~B. Make it better somehow.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. Just guess the best answer.#Guessing does not support understanding.
=D. Identify the audience, choose the tone, then explain your reason.#Correct. This gives specific steps.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which response has the weakest tone?
{
=A. That idea is terrible and useless.#Correct. This tone is disrespectful and would likely harm discussion.
~B. I see your point, but I have another concern.#This is respectful disagreement.
~C. Your idea connects to our purpose.#This is positive and focused.
~D. Could we clarify the audience first?#This is respectful and useful.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence best replaces "The speech thing is good"?
{
=A. The opening question helps the audience focus on the claim.#Correct. This is precise and explains the communication effect.
~B. The speech thing is very good.#This remains vague.
~C. The stuff works.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~D. It is good because it is good.#This gives no explanation.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best supports collaboration?
{
~A. I will decide for everyone.#This removes collaboration.
=B. Let's compare both openings and choose the one that fits our audience.#Correct. This invites shared decision-making.
~C. Nobody else should talk.#This blocks participation.
~D. We should ignore the audience.#Audience is central to communication.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "As a peer mentor, I have helped new students prepare for presentations." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Emotion because the speaker mentions feelings#No feeling is described.
~B. Logic because the speaker gives a statistic#No statistic is given.
=C. Trust because the speaker shows relevant experience#Correct. Relevant experience builds credibility.
~D. No appeal because it is one sentence#One sentence can still use a rhetorical appeal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which choice best summarizes audience-aware communication?
{
~A. Say whatever sounds strongest.#Strength alone is not enough.
~B. Use the same tone for everyone.#Different audiences may need different tone.
~C. Ignore purpose and focus on volume.#Purpose matters more than volume.
=D. Choose words, tone, and support that fit the audience and purpose.#Correct. Audience-aware communication is intentional and purposeful.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L07_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which statement best summarizes Lesson 7?
{
=A. Strong communication combines audience awareness, listening, tone, academic language, and purpose.#Correct. Lesson 7 integrates the Unit 1 skills.
~B. Strong communication uses only tone.#Tone matters, but it is only one skill.
~C. Strong communication ignores audience.#Audience awareness is essential.
~D. Strong communication avoids listening.#Listening supports accurate response.
}
